Course 5: Lower-body power (Detailed Research)

Category: Strength and conditioning
Duration: 30 min
Level: 3/5
What the Course Should Cover

This course focuses on squats, lunges, and plyometric jumps to improve acceleration and jumping. Lower-body power is described by FIFA as "an essential attribute for athleticism" that "transfers forces up the kinetic chain (specifically the ankle, knee and hip muscles and joints), which is vital to producing powerful locomotion in dynamic situations and for physical competitiveness". The kinetic chain in the lower body enables players to "shift their bodies dynamically and efficiently," which is crucial for "challenges for the ball, holding off an opponent, acceleration to deceleration and changes of direction".
Key Coaching Points
Foundational Strength First

    Master fundamental movement patterns with bodyweight before adding load — squats, lunges, and basic jumps should be performed with excellent technique before progressing

    The kinetic chain must be highly coordinated — lower-body strength enables energy transfer from the legs to the rest of the body

    Combine with core strength and mobility — these attributes "work in harmony" to benefit performance, as "mobility facilitates ease and efficiency of movement, while a strong core absorbs and stabilises against force"

Squat Technique

    Keep the head up and chest out, maintain the back in a neutral position

    Touch the knee lightly against the floor (for split squats) and drive through glutes and quads upon return

    Progress to unilateral variations — single-leg squats and Bulgarian split squats require greater coordination and stability

Lunge Technique

    Lower under control, drive back upwards powerfully

    Maintain stability and alignment throughout the movement

    Progression options: Forward lunge with rotational twist (resisted), which challenges "the lower-body kinetic chain and deep lumbar and abdominal core muscles"

Plyometric Jump Technique

    Land softly on the anterior part of the foot

    Control the landing cleanly and finish in a stable, set position

    For reactive jumps: Upon landing, "instantaneously use the landing load to recoil and drive upwards"

Common Mistakes to Address
Mistake	Why It Happens	Correction
Knees collapse inward during squat	Weak glute activation	Emphasize hip abduction; keep knees tracking over toes
Heavy landing on plyometrics	Poor eccentric control	Land softly on the ball of the foot; absorb force through ankle, knee, hip
Progressing to unilateral too early	Insufficient stability	Only advance when bilateral exercises are performed "perfectly"
Adding load before technique is mastered	Eagerness to progress	"Increase the resistance, but only once the exercise has been performed perfectly"
Neglecting single-leg work	Focus only on bilateral lifts	"Both bilateral and unilateral exercises provide a spectrum of explosive loading stimuli"
Sample Exercises (from FIFA Training Centre)
Exercise 1: Kettlebell Split Squats / Lunge

    Target: Quadriceps, glutes, hamstrings, deep abdominals, hip adductors, calf muscles

    Execution: Keep head up and chest out, maintain neutral back. Touch knee lightly against the floor and drive through glutes and quads upon return

    Progression: Increase resistance once perfect form achieved, or elevate front leg on a small step

Exercise 2: Alternating Reverse Split Squats / Lunge

    Target: Same as above, "now requiring increased stability and core activation"

    Progression: "Alternately raise the kettlebell and hold it at shoulder height with elbows high, which will increase the engagement of the core"

Exercise 3: Bulgarian Split Squats

    Target: "Increased unilateral focus and requiring more strength, better coordination and greater stability"

    Execution: "Tilt slightly forwards as you lower and drive back upwards. This will increase the function of the exercise through forward propulsion"

Exercise 4: Single-leg Skater Squats

    Target: "Complete unilateral strength" with focus on "leg strength, core stability and spinal mobility"

    Execution: "Use a box, step or other elevated platform at the correct height in order to reach the biting point (the point at which you would lose control if you went any lower)"

    Progression: "Gradually lower the height of the box... Only increase the range of motion once the exercise has been performed perfectly"

Exercise 5: Seated to Vertical Jump (Bilateral)

    Load type: Static and low

    Focus: "Bilateral variation emphasises collective triple extension (ankle, knee and hip) to develop explosive force from a static, concentric, uncoiled position"

    Execution: "Sit tall, with a long, strong spine, keep your head up and the feet shoulder-width apart. Lift your feet two to four inches off the floor, drive them instantly back into the floor, and power up vertically, using an explosive arm swing for momentum"

Exercise 6: Seated to Vertical and Lateral Jumps (Bilateral)

    Load type: Static to elastic and medium

    Focus: "Adds a reactive countermovement into the equation. Upon landing from the initial vertical jump, players must instantaneously absorb the force and jump laterally for height and distance"

    Execution: "Upon landing, try to use the force from the vertical jump to instantly recoil and drive laterally in one continuous movement"

Exercise 7: Single-leg Lateral to Vertical Jumps (Unilateral)

    Load type: Elastic and high

    Focus: "A demanding, high-loading single-leg variation that requires a sharp transfer of force from a lateral jump for distance directly into a vertical jump for height off the landing leg"

    Execution: "Upon impact from the lateral jump, instantaneously use the landing load to recoil and drive upwards vertically off the single leg. Land cleanly, hold your balance and repeat the sequence on the opposite leg"

Session Structure Suggestion (30 minutes)
Time	Activity	Focus
5 min	Dynamic warm-up (leg swings, hip rotations, light jogging)	Joint mobility, muscle activation
8 min	Strength block: Split squats or lunges (bilateral)	Foundational strength, technique
7 min	Unilateral block: Bulgarian split squats or skater squats	Balance, stability, single-leg strength
5 min	Plyometric block: Seated to vertical jumps, lateral jumps	Explosive power, landing mechanics
5 min	Cool-down: Light stretching, foam rolling	Recovery, flexibility

Note: The FIFA Training Centre emphasizes that players should only progress to more advanced exercises once excellent control and technique has been achieved in the simpler variations.
Assessment Indicators

A player has improved when they:

    Can perform bilateral and unilateral squats/lunges with perfect technique before adding load

    Lands softly and controls the landing during plyometric jumps

    Demonstrates improved acceleration and jumping in match situations

    Maintains stability and alignment during single-leg exercises

